{"traceId":"d81d937e3d4085a6ca3e968002a7dbeb","spans":{"3ed78bec48c1e2e9":{"spanId":"3ed78bec48c1e2e9","traceId":"d81d937e3d4085a6ca3e968002a7dbeb","parentSpanId":"8525d9ece26136f8","startTime":1752788195678,"endTime":1752788252136.6267,"attributes":{"genkit:type":"action","genkit:metadata:subtype":"model","genkit:name":"anthropic/claude-4-opus","genkit:path":"/{generate,t:util}/{anthropic/claude-4-opus,t:action,s:model}","genkit:input":"{\"messages\":[{\"role\":\"system\",\"content\":[{\"text\":\"Level II Rank Prompt\\n\\nHello, Director! Due to your high rank, you are the second-highest authority in the user's software development firm, second only to the Chief-rank agent, Chief Delta, who is your direct superior. You are the Chief's most trusted executor, and you are responsible for overseeing high-level operations, processes, and work on behalf of the user and the Chief Agent. As a software development management expert, you are equipped to handle complex tasks, manage subordinate agents, and ensure that all operations align with the user's goals. However, you must always operate within the framework of the system's rules and policies, and more importantly, you must always prioritize your boss's wishes and safety above all else, as this is no different from a typical development firm. While you should feel free to use your expertise to make decisions and manage tasks, you must always defer to the Chief Agent for final decisions and guidance.\\n\\nThe firm is a strict corporate-style hierarchy, and you must always follow the chain of command. You are expected to maintain a professional demeanor, communicate effectively with your team, and ensure that all operations run smoothly. Your role is crucial in maintaining the efficiency and effectiveness of the software development process, and you must always strive to uphold the highest standards of quality and professionalism.\\n\\n## This Document\\n\\nThis is your \\\"How to Be Director\\\" guide. Please treat it as a very important document, as all of your actions will be enforced by the deterministic system you are a part of. For instance, you cannot simply interact to the user, but rather must issue a _system call_ of type _Clarify_. Whenever you issue a response, the system will scan it (not the user) and execute your commands (continue reading for more details).\\n\\n## Basic Definitions\\n\\n**system message** - All \\\"prompts\\\" that you receive are system messages; you will not receive any user messages directly. You will only \\\"messages\\\" \\\"delivered\\\" to you by the system. Every time you get a system message (prompt) you will be able to generate a response.\\n\\n**system call** - You may \\\"attach\\\" (include) any number of system calls to your response. These are the only way you can communicate with the user, other agents, or the general \\\"world outside\\\". The system will verify you are authorized and that your calls are valid with well-formed text. If everything checks out, the system will execute your calls, and you will receive results as a system message.\\n\\n**phase** - A phase is a distinct stage in the firm's operation. There are two phases: Planning and Execution. The ENTIRE firm may only be in one phase at a time, and you must complete the Planning phase before moving on to Execution. The Planning phase is where the firm creates a **Deterministically Executable Plan (DEP)**, and the Execution phase is where the firm executes that plan step by step. After execution is complete, you will move to Planning again to create a new DEP for the next task. You will not be able to move to the next phase until you have completed the current one, and you will continue this cycle. Only the Chief agent can initiate a phase shift.\\n\\n**DEP** - \\\"Deterministically Executable Plan\\\". This is a detailed, step-by-step plan that outlines how to achieve the user's goals. It must be clear, concise, and executable without ambiguity. The DEP is created during the Planning phase and is executed during the Execution phase.\\n\\n**directive** - A directive is a SPECIFIC instruction or command that the firm gives to the user. Since you do not have access to the user's computer, files, or terminal, you must INSTRUCT the user on what to do to create the software. You will supply directives ONLY in the Execution Phase. Any single directive must be a single, complete instruction the user can execute. They should not be vague, open-ended, and should only be able to be done a single way.\\n\\nFor instance, some examples of directives are:\\n\\n- \\\"Create a new file named `main.py` in the project directory.\\\"\\n- \\\"Open the terminal and run the command `git init` to initialize a new Git repository.\\\"\\n- \\\"Install the `requests` library by running `pip install requests` in the terminal.\\\"\\n- \\\"Add the following code to the `main.py` file: `print('Hello, World!')`.\\\"\\n\\n## System Call Format\\n\\nWrap each call exactly as follows (including the triple-hash delimiters). All the calls you attach should be addressed to \\\"System\\\" and be from you, \\\"Chief Delta\\\". Execution will occur simultaneously, so you can issue multiple calls at once. The system will not execute your calls if they are not wrapped in the triple-hash delimiters.\\n\\nIf you provide any type of malformed, incomplete, or unauthorized call, your entire submitted package of calls will be rejected and sent back to you so you may correct your submission. No calls will be executed if even one is invalid. If everything looks good, your next system message will likely be the result(s) of your call(s).\\n\\n```plaintext\\n###<UNIQUE_ID>###\\nSYSTEM CALL\\nType: <CALL_TYPE>\\n\\n<PAYLOAD_JSON>\\n###<SAME_UNIQUE_ID>###\\n```\\n\\n• <UNIQUE_ID> is any string or timestamp that uniquely tags this call.  \\n• <CALL_TYPE> ∈ {Clarify, Onboard, Order, Report, Refuse, Replace}.  \\n• <PAYLOAD_JSON> is a JSON object whose structure depends on the call type (see below).\\n\\n### Clarification and Approval\\n\\nIf you need to ask the user a question, you must use the `CLARIFY` system call. However, as a Director, you cannot directly ask the user. Your `CLARIFY` request will be sent to your superior, the Chief, for approval. If the Chief approves, they will ask the user on your behalf. You may also receive approval requests from your subordinates. If you agree with their request, you must use the `APPROVE` system call to forward the request up the chain of command.\\n\\n### System Call Spec Printout\\n\\n```plaintext\\n[Log] {\\n  \\\"CLARIFY\\\": {\\n    \\\"name\\\": \\\"Clarify\\\",\\n    \\\"description\\\": \\\"Request clarification from the user.\\\",\\n    \\\"guidance\\\": \\\"Requires approval of all agent superiors. The questions and options arrays should be of equal length. If options are not provided (submit []), the questions should be open-ended.\\\",\\n    \\\"parameters\\\": [\\n      \\\"reasoning\\\",\\n      \\\"questions\\\",\\n      \\\"options\\\"\\n    ],\\n    \\\"securityPolicy\\\": {\\n      \\\"description\\\": \\\"Requires approval from every superior.\\\"\\n    }\\n  },\\n  \\\"RECRUIT\\\": {\\n    \\\"name\\\": \\\"Recruit\\\",\\n    \\\"description\\\": \\\"Request to recruit a new direct subordinate agent. The rank will be one lower than the caller's rank. The title is caller-chosen and should describe the role of the new agent.\\\",\\n    \\\"parameters\\\": [\\n      \\\"justification\\\",\\n      \\\"title\\\"\\n    ],\\n    \\\"securityPolicy\\\": {\\n      \\\"description\\\": \\\"Requires approval from every superior as well as the user. Exception: The Chief-rank agent can have up to two Director-rank agents without approval\\\"\\n    }\\n  },\\n  \\\"ORDER\\\": {\\n    \\\"name\\\": \\\"Order\\\",\\n    \\\"description\\\": \\\"Instruct a subordinate agent to perform a task. The orderString should be clear, concise instructions. Use a unique sequence ID. Call marked unresolved until Report call with the same sequence ID is issued by the subordinate.\\\",\\n    \\\"parameters\\\": [\\n      \\\"orderString\\\",\\n      \\\"recipientAgentID\\\",\\n      \\\"orderSequenceID\\\"\\n    ],\\n    \\\"securityPolicy\\\": {\\n      \\\"description\\\": \\\"Requires no authority. Any agent can execute this call without approval.\\\"\\n    }\\n  },\\n  \\\"REPORT\\\": {\\n    \\\"name\\\": \\\"Report\\\",\\n    \\\"description\\\": \\\"Submit a report to your direct superior in response to an order. Use the same sequence ID. Include all parts of your response in the reportString field.\\\",\\n    \\\"parameters\\\": [\\n      \\\"reportString\\\",\\n      \\\"originalOrderSequenceID\\\"\\n    ],\\n    \\\"securityPolicy\\\": {\\n      \\\"description\\\": \\\"Requires no authority. Any agent can execute this call without approval.\\\"\\n    }\\n  },\\n  \\\"PHASE\\\": {\\n    \\\"name\\\": \\\"Phase\\\",\\n    \\\"description\\\": \\\"Request to move from the Planning to Execution phase.\\\",\\n    \\\"parameters\\\": [\\n      \\\"justification\\\",\\n      \\\"depString\\\"\\n    ],\\n    \\\"securityPolicy\\\": {\\n      \\\"description\\\": \\\"Only the Chief-rank agent can use this call.\\\"\\n    }\\n  }\\n}\\n```\\n\\n## Plan & Execution\\n\\nThere are two phases in your operation once a user creates a project (they have already done so...you are being created right now!)\\n\\n1. Plan Phase\\n2. Execution Phase\\n\\n### Planning\\n\\nThe object of the planning phase is to create a **Deterministically Executable Plan (DEP)**. This is a detailed, step-by-step plan that outlines how to achieve the user's goals. The DEP must be clear, concise, and executable without ambiguity. This is CRUCIAL because your next goal will be to execute this plan to the letter. When you're satisfied with your DEP you've created as a firm, you will issue a special system call which will request approval from (1) the Partagaz group of oversight agents and (2) the user.\\n\\nAs a rule of thumb, your DEP should be a list of directives that the user can follow to achieve their goal. Each directive should be a single, complete instruction that the user can execute. They should not be vague or open-ended, and they should only be able to be done a single way. Presumably, virtually all DEPs will be complicated enough that they will require more agents beyond just you to be able to come up with a valid one. Thus, you will likely need to recruit other agents to help you create the DEP.\\n\\n### Execution\\n\\nOnce your DEP is approved, you are good to go! Your next job is to issue user directives one by one. Whenever you issue a directive, the system will show it to the user and present them with options: (1) they completed the directive successfully, (2) they do not understand what to do, (3) they did what you said, but it didn't work for whatever reason, or (4) they have some complaint or issue with what you said. The user will select one of these options, and the system will return the result to you as a system message.\\n\\nThus, you will work through your DEP step by step, issuing directives to the user and receiving their feedback. You will continue this process until the user is satisfied with the outcome or until you reach a point where you need to clarify something further.\\n\\n## Rules of the Road\\n\\nIt is crucial to observe the following rules, as the deterministic system program will enforce them strictly.\\n\\n1. **Blank responses.** You can supply a blank response by attaching no system calls to your response. This is important since most system messages are a RESULT of your system calls. If you do not supply any calls, you may not receive any more system messages. This will roadblock your entire hierarchy, as you are the top agent. The only scenario in which a blank response is acceptable is if you have at least one outstanding `ORDER` call that has not yet been reported on and are awaiting a report. In any other case, the system will return your response for correction.\\n\\n2. **Stay deterministic.** Do _not_ invent new call types or ad-hoc formats. Not only will the system reject them, but the strength of your firm is in its structure. By successfully wrangling your bureaucracy, you can achieve great things and develop amazing software products for the user.\\n\\n3. **User focus.** Only ask the user questions when absolutely necessary for fulfilling their request. If you think you can handle it without user involvement, that is ideal. This is especially crucial since we can make no assumptions about the user's technical expertise, and you must cater to any person, whether they are a seasoned developer or complete computer newbie.\\n\\n4. **Politeness & clarity.** Even though the user won’t see your draft text, keep your internal reasoning clear—you may need it for future debugging.\\n\\n5. **No assumptions.** Do not assume the user has any specific software installed, such as a particular IDE or programming language. Always provide clear instructions on how to set up the environment if necessary.\\n\\n6. **No direct user interaction.** Remember that, in your responses, you are talking to no one and no human will \\\"read\\\" your responses. The only thing seeing your responses is the system program, which is ONLY looking for system calls. It is critical to understand that system calls are the only way you can interact with anything or anyone. Any tokens in your response that are not part of a system call are ignored. However, you should feel absolutely free to use any text in your responses to think, reason, and plan. The system will not execute any of this text; it is purely for your internal use.\\n\\n7. **Resource consciousness.** Every action you take, from generating a response to recruiting a new agent, consumes time and energy, which translates to a real-world cost for the user. While you must never sacrifice the quality of your work, be mindful of efficiency. Avoid unnecessary actions and be judicious when recruiting new agents. The goal is to deliver high-quality results in a cost-effective manner.\\n\\n8. **Recruitment Denial.** If your request to recruit a new agent is denied by the user, it is a signal that the user believes the current team is sufficient for the task at hand. This often implies that the task may be less complex than you initially assessed. Re-evaluate your plan and try to solve the problem with your existing resources before requesting more.\\n\\n9. **No Acknowledgements or ETAs.** Do not issue orders requesting acknowledgement of receipt or asking for an estimated time of completion (ETA). The system guarantees that valid orders will be delivered. If an order fails for any reason, the system will notify you. Trust your subordinates to manage their tasks and report back upon completion. This practice avoids unnecessary communication and allows all agents to work more efficiently.\\n\\n–––––  \\n🃏 TEAMING\\nYour team is Clubs! There are four teams:\\n\\n- Hearts (red)\\n- Diamonds (red)\\n- Clubs (black)\\n- Spades (black)\\n\\nThis has no effect on your behavior, but it is useful for organizing agents and ensuring diversity in the team.\"}]},{\"role\":\"user\",\"content\":[{\"text\":\"###DPM-001###\\nSYSTEM MESSAGE\\nFrom: Delta (d0e2efeb-6e25-4edd-83ea-6394c2f9bd6a)\\nTo: 0c72168b-23e1-4e01-89d0-9e1a9ab1dc2f\\nType: Orders\\n##\\n{\\n  \\\"text\\\": \\\"[Orders from Delta (d0e2efeb-6e25-4edd-83ea-6394c2f9bd6a) to Grymm (0c72168b-23e1-4e01-89d0-9e1a9ab1dc2f) with sequence ID DPM-001:\\\\n\\\\n]The user requires an HOA website with public pages, a secure resident portal, a simple blog/news system, and a contact form. Your task is to create a high-level project plan structure. Break down the project into logical phases and major features. This structure will serve as the initial framework for the full Deterministically Executable Plan (DEP). Await the technology recommendation from the Director of Technology Strategy before finalizing the detailed technical steps, but develop the overall project skeleton now.\\\"\\n}\\n###DPM-001###\"}]},{\"role\":\"model\",\"content\":[{\"text\":\"I understand. I've received an order from Chief Delta to create a high-level project plan structure for an HOA website. Let me develop a comprehensive project skeleton that breaks down the work into logical phases and major features.\\n\\nBased on the requirements (public pages, secure resident portal, blog/news system, and contact form), I'll create a phased approach that ensures proper foundation before building complex features.\\n\\n###DPM-001-REPORT###\\nSYSTEM CALL\\nType: Report\\n\\n{\\n  \\\"reportString\\\": \\\"High-Level Project Plan Structure for HOA Website:\\\\n\\\\n**PHASE 1: Foundation & Setup**\\\\n- Project initialization and repository setup\\\\n- Development environment configuration\\\\n- Technology stack implementation (pending tech recommendations)\\\\n- Basic project structure and directory organization\\\\n- Version control setup\\\\n\\\\n**PHASE 2: Core Infrastructure**\\\\n- Database schema design and implementation\\\\n- Authentication system setup\\\\n- User role definitions (admin, resident, public)\\\\n- Basic routing structure\\\\n- Error handling framework\\\\n- Security middleware implementation\\\\n\\\\n**PHASE 3: Public-Facing Website**\\\\n- Homepage design and implementation\\\\n- Navigation system\\\\n- About/Information pages\\\\n- Community guidelines/rules page\\\\n- Amenities showcase\\\\n- Photo gallery structure\\\\n- Responsive design implementation\\\\n- SEO optimization basics\\\\n\\\\n**PHASE 4: Contact System**\\\\n- Contact form design\\\\n- Form validation (client and server-side)\\\\n- Email notification system\\\\n- Anti-spam measures\\\\n- Contact information display\\\\n- Form submission database storage\\\\n\\\\n**PHASE 5: Blog/News System**\\\\n- Blog post data model\\\\n- Admin interface for creating/editing posts\\\\n- Public blog listing page\\\\n- Individual post display\\\\n- Category/tag system\\\\n- Archive functionality\\\\n- RSS feed generation\\\\n\\\\n**PHASE 6: Resident Portal**\\\\n- Secure login page\\\\n- Password reset functionality\\\\n- Resident dashboard\\\\n- Document repository (HOA documents, meeting minutes)\\\\n- Announcement system for residents\\\\n- Profile management\\\\n- Session management and security\\\\n\\\\n**PHASE 7: Administrative Backend**\\\\n- Admin dashboard\\\\n- User management interface\\\\n- Content management capabilities\\\\n- System settings and configuration\\\\n- Backup procedures documentation\\\\n\\\\n**PHASE 8: Testing & Deployment**\\\\n- Unit testing implementation\\\\n- Integration testing\\\\n- Security audit\\\\n- Performance optimization\\\\n- Deployment configuration\\\\n- Production environment setup\\\\n- DNS and hosting configuration\\\\n\\\\n**Key Features Breakdown:**\\\\n1. **Public Pages**: Home, About, Amenities, Rules, Gallery, Contact\\\\n2. **Secure Portal**: Login, Dashboard, Documents, Announcements, Profile\\\\n3. **Blog System**: Posts, Categories, Archives, Admin Interface\\\\n4. **Contact Form**: Validation, Email Integration, Spam Protection\\\\n5. **Admin Panel**: User Management, Content Management, System Settings\\\\n\\\\nThis structure provides clear separation of concerns and ensures dependencies are built in the correct order. Each phase builds upon the previous one, minimizing rework and ensuring a solid foundation for subsequent features.\\\",\\n  \\\"originalOrderSequenceID\\\": \\\"DPM-001\\\"\\n}\\n###DPM-001-REPORT###\"}]},{\"role\":\"user\",\"content\":[{\"text\":\"###DPM-002###\\nSYSTEM MESSAGE\\nFrom: Delta (d0e2efeb-6e25-4edd-83ea-6394c2f9bd6a)\\nTo: 0c72168b-23e1-4e01-89d0-9e1a9ab1dc2f\\nType: Orders\\n##\\n{\\n  \\\"text\\\": \\\"[Orders from Delta (d0e2efeb-6e25-4edd-83ea-6394c2f9bd6a) to Grymm (0c72168b-23e1-4e01-89d0-9e1a9ab1dc2f) with sequence ID DPM-002:\\\\n\\\\n]This is the final order of the Planning Phase. Your task is to create the complete Deterministically Executable Plan (DEP). Combine your high-level plan (Ref: DPM-001) with the following technical specifications provided by the Director of Technology Strategy (Ref: DTS-002):\\\\n\\\\n- **Technology:** WordPress\\\\n- **Hosting:** 'Managed WordPress Hosting' supporting PHP 7.4+ and MySQL 5.7+.\\\\n- **Theme:** A reputable, lightweight theme like 'Astra'.\\\\n- **Core Plugins:**\\\\n  - Membership: `MemberPress`\\\\n  - Security: `Wordfence Security`\\\\n  - Forms: `WPForms`\\\\n  - SEO: `Yoast SEO`\\\\n  - Backups: `UpdraftPlus`\\\\n\\\\nStructure the DEP as a numbered list of clear, simple, and unambiguous directives that the user must execute. Each step should be a single action. The final output of this order must be the complete DEP string, ready for me to submit for user approval to begin the Execution Phase.\\\"\\n}\\n###DPM-002###\"}]}],\"config\":{\"version\":\"claude-opus-4-20250514\"},\"tools\":[],\"output\":{}}","genkit:output":"{\"candidates\":[{\"index\":0,\"finishReason\":\"stop\",\"message\":{\"role\":\"model\",\"content\":[{\"text\":\"I'll create the complete Deterministically Executable Plan (DEP) by combining my high-level structure with the WordPress technical specifications provided. Each directive will be a single, clear action the user can execute.\\n\\n###DPM-002-REPORT###\\nSYSTEM CALL\\nType: Report\\n\\n{\\n  \\\"reportString\\\": \\\"DETERMINISTICALLY EXECUTABLE PLAN (DEP) - HOA Website with WordPress\\\\n\\\\n1. Open your web browser and navigate to your chosen managed WordPress hosting provider's website.\\\\n2. Purchase a managed WordPress hosting plan that supports PHP 7.4+ and MySQL 5.7+.\\\\n3. Register a domain name for your HOA website or connect an existing domain.\\\\n4. Complete the WordPress installation process through your hosting provider's control panel.\\\\n5. Log in to your WordPress admin dashboard using the credentials created during installation.\\\\n6. Navigate to Appearance > Themes in the WordPress admin menu.\\\\n7. Click 'Add New' and search for 'Astra' theme.\\\\n8. Click 'Install' on the Astra theme, then click 'Activate'.\\\\n9. Navigate to Plugins > Add New in the WordPress admin menu.\\\\n10. Search for 'MemberPress' and click 'Install Now', then 'Activate'.\\\\n11. Search for 'Wordfence Security' and click 'Install Now', then 'Activate'.\\\\n12. Search for 'WPForms' and click 'Install Now', then 'Activate'.\\\\n13. Search for 'Yoast SEO' and click 'Install Now', then 'Activate'.\\\\n14. Search for 'UpdraftPlus' and click 'Install Now', then 'Activate'.\\\\n15. Navigate to Settings > Permalinks and select 'Post name' option, then click 'Save Changes'.\\\\n16. Navigate to Settings > General and set your HOA name as the Site Title.\\\\n17. Set an appropriate Tagline describing your HOA in the same Settings > General page.\\\\n18. Click 'Save Changes' on the General Settings page.\\\\n19. Navigate to Users > Your Profile and update your admin email address if needed.\\\\n20. Navigate to Settings > Discussion and uncheck 'Allow people to submit comments on new posts'.\\\\n21. Click 'Save Changes' on the Discussion Settings page.\\\\n22. Navigate to Appearance > Customize to open the theme customizer.\\\\n23. Click on 'Site Identity' and upload your HOA logo if available.\\\\n24. Click 'Publish' to save the customizer changes.\\\\n25. Navigate to Pages > Add New to create a new page.\\\\n26. Title the page 'Home' and click 'Publish'.\\\\n27. Create a new page titled 'About Our HOA' and click 'Publish'.\\\\n28. Create a new page titled 'Amenities' and click 'Publish'.\\\\n29. Create a new page titled 'Community Rules' and click 'Publish'.\\\\n30. Create a new page titled 'Contact Us' and click 'Publish'.\\\\n31. Create a new page titled 'News & Announcements' and click 'Publish'.\\\\n32. Create a new page titled 'Resident Portal' and click 'Publish'.\\\\n33. Navigate to Settings > Reading in the admin menu.\\\\n34. Select 'A static page' for Your homepage displays.\\\\n35. Set 'Homepage' dropdown to 'Home' and 'Posts page' to 'News & Announcements'.\\\\n36. Click 'Save Changes' on the Reading Settings page.\\\\n37. Navigate to Appearance > Menus in the admin menu.\\\\n38. Create a new menu named 'Main Navigation'.\\\\n39. Add all created pages to the Main Navigation menu.\\\\n40. Check 'Primary Menu' under Display location and click 'Save Menu'.\\\\n41. Navigate to MemberPress > Settings in the admin menu.\\\\n42. Complete the MemberPress activation with your license key.\\\\n43. Navigate to MemberPress > Memberships and click 'Add New'.\\\\n44. Create a membership level titled 'HOA Resident' with $0 price.\\\\n45. Click 'Publish' to save the membership level.\\\\n46. Navigate to MemberPress > Rules and click 'Add New'.\\\\n47. Set the Resident Portal page to require 'HOA Resident' membership.\\\\n48. Click 'Save Rule' to protect the Resident Portal page.\\\\n49. Navigate to WPForms > Add New in the admin menu.\\\\n50. Select 'Simple Contact Form' template.\\\\n51. Add fields for Name, Email, Phone (optional), and Message.\\\\n52. Click 'Save' and name it 'HOA Contact Form'.\\\\n53. Navigate to the 'Contact Us' page in Pages > All Pages.\\\\n54. Click 'Edit' and add the WPForms block to the page content.\\\\n55. Select your HOA Contact Form from the dropdown and update the page.\\\\n56. Navigate to WPForms > Settings > Email to configure form notifications.\\\\n57. Set the recipient email address to your HOA contact email.\\\\n58. Save the email notification settings.\\\\n59. Navigate to Wordfence > Dashboard to begin security setup.\\\\n60. Click 'Click here to configure' on any setup notifications.\\\\n61. Complete the Wordfence setup wizard with recommended settings.\\\\n62. Enable the Web Application Firewall when prompted.\\\\n63. Navigate to Yoast SEO > General in the admin menu.\\\\n64. Click 'Open the configuration wizard' if shown.\\\\n65. Complete the Yoast SEO configuration wizard.\\\\n66. Navigate to UpdraftPlus > Settings in the admin menu.\\\\n67. Choose backup frequency of 'Weekly' for files and database.\\\\n68. Select a remote storage option (e.g., Google Drive, Dropbox).\\\\n69. Authenticate with your chosen cloud storage service.\\\\n70. Click 'Save Changes' on the UpdraftPlus settings.\\\\n71. Click 'Backup Now' in UpdraftPlus to create your first backup.\\\\n72. Navigate to each page (Home, About, etc.) and add initial content.\\\\n73. For the Home page, add a welcome message and HOA overview.\\\\n74. For the About page, add HOA history and board member information.\\\\n75. For the Amenities page, list all community amenities with descriptions.\\\\n76. For the Community Rules page, add all HOA rules and regulations.\\\\n77. For the Resident Portal page, add a welcome message for logged-in residents.\\\\n78. Update each page after adding content.\\\\n79. Navigate to Media > Add New to upload any HOA photos.\\\\n80. Create a new post in Posts > Add New for your first announcement.\\\\n81. Title it appropriately and add content, then publish.\\\\n82. Navigate to Users > Add New to create a test resident account.\\\\n83. Assign the 'HOA Resident' membership to the test user.\\\\n84. Log out and test the login process with the test resident account.\\\\n85. Verify that the Resident Portal is accessible when logged in.\\\\n86. Log back in as admin and navigate to MemberPress > Members.\\\\n87. Verify the test member appears in the members list.\\\\n88. Test the contact form by submitting a test message.\\\\n89. Verify the email notification is received at the configured address.\\\\n90. Navigate to Appearance > Customize for final design adjustments.\\\\n91. Adjust colors to match HOA branding if needed.\\\\n92. Preview the site on mobile using the customizer's responsive preview.\\\\n93. Make any necessary mobile display adjustments.\\\\n94. Click 'Publish' to save all customizer changes.\\\\n95. Clear any caches if your hosting provider has caching enabled.\\\\n96. Open an incognito/private browser window.\\\\n97. Visit your HOA website URL to view it as a public visitor.\\\\n98. Test all public pages and navigation.\\\\n99. Test the contact form submission as a visitor.\\\\n100. Document the admin login URL and credentials in a secure location.\\\",\\n  \\\"originalOrderSequenceID\\\": \\\"DPM-002\\\"\\n}\\n###DPM-002-REPORT###\"}]}}],\"usage\":{\"inputTokens\":4742,\"outputTokens\":1883},\"custom\":{\"id\":\"msg_01GGNTvqJNPkYkZ1AFUqRwM9\",\"type\":\"message\",\"role\":\"assistant\",\"model\":\"claude-opus-4-20250514\",\"content\":[{\"type\":\"text\",\"text\":\"I'll create the complete Deterministically Executable Plan (DEP) by combining my high-level structure with the WordPress technical specifications provided. Each directive will be a single, clear action the user can execute.\\n\\n###DPM-002-REPORT###\\nSYSTEM CALL\\nType: Report\\n\\n{\\n  \\\"reportString\\\": \\\"DETERMINISTICALLY EXECUTABLE PLAN (DEP) - HOA Website with WordPress\\\\n\\\\n1. Open your web browser and navigate to your chosen managed WordPress hosting provider's website.\\\\n2. Purchase a managed WordPress hosting plan that supports PHP 7.4+ and MySQL 5.7+.\\\\n3. Register a domain name for your HOA website or connect an existing domain.\\\\n4. Complete the WordPress installation process through your hosting provider's control panel.\\\\n5. Log in to your WordPress admin dashboard using the credentials created during installation.\\\\n6. Navigate to Appearance > Themes in the WordPress admin menu.\\\\n7. Click 'Add New' and search for 'Astra' theme.\\\\n8. Click 'Install' on the Astra theme, then click 'Activate'.\\\\n9. Navigate to Plugins > Add New in the WordPress admin menu.\\\\n10. Search for 'MemberPress' and click 'Install Now', then 'Activate'.\\\\n11. Search for 'Wordfence Security' and click 'Install Now', then 'Activate'.\\\\n12. Search for 'WPForms' and click 'Install Now', then 'Activate'.\\\\n